proofs.proof_tex:
Let
\[
 A_{n,P}=\left\{
 \sup_{\theta\in\Theta_I(P)}
 \frac{\sqrt n\{\widehat q_n(\theta)-q_P(\theta)\}}
      {\widehat\sigma_n(\theta)\vee n^{-1}}
 \leq\widehat c_{1-\eta}\right\}. \tag{1}
\]
For every \(\theta\in\Theta_I(P)\), the sharp score characterization gives \(q_P(\theta)\leq0\).  On \(A_{n,P}\),
\[
 \frac{\sqrt n\widehat q_n(\theta)}
      {\widehat\sigma_n(\theta)\vee n^{-1}}
 \leq
 \frac{\sqrt n\{\widehat q_n(\theta)-q_P(\theta)\}}
      {\widehat\sigma_n(\theta)\vee n^{-1}}
 \leq\widehat c_{1-\eta}.
\]
Thus \(\theta\in C_n\), and consequently \(A_{n,P}\subseteq
\{\Theta_I(P)\subseteq C_n\}\).  Assertion (A) of the affirmative multiplier-coupling theorem therefore yields
\[
 \liminf_{n\to\infty}\inf_{P\in
 \mathcal P_n^{\mathrm{ct}}(C_\delta)\cup
 \mathcal P^{\mathrm{int}}(\delta_0)}
 P\{\Theta_I(P)\subseteq C_n\}\geq1-\eta. \tag{2}
\]
This proves whole-identified-set coverage, not merely coverage of a selected structural point.

For loss, take \(\mathcal E_{n,P}\) and \(R_{n,P}\) from (B)--(C) of that theorem.  The endpoint-domain lemma and the structural restrictions make \(\Theta_I(P)\) nonempty throughout \(\mathcal P_W\).  Since both \(C_n\) and \(\Theta_I(P)\) lie in compact \(K\), the empty-set convention gives
\[
 0\leq d_{\to}(C_n,\Theta_I(P))\leq\operatorname{diam}(K) \tag{3}
\]
on every sample, including samples on which \(C_n=\varnothing\).

On \(\mathcal E_{n,P}\), condition (C) places \(C_n\) inside the relaxed score sublevel set with error radius \(R_{n,P}\).  If \(P\in\mathcal P_n^{\mathrm{ct}}(C_\delta)\), then
\[
 0\leq\delta(P)\leq C_\delta n^{-1/2}
 \leq C_\delta R_{n,P}.
\]
The contact part of the deterministic score-error modulus and Jensen's inequality imply
\[
 \begin{split}
 E_Pd_{\to}(C_n,\Theta_I(P))
 &\leq C E_P[\sqrt{R_{n,P}}]
 +\operatorname{diam}(K)P(\mathcal E_{n,P}^c)\\
 &\leq C\sqrt{E_PR_{n,P}}+O(n^{-2})
 =O(n^{-1/4}), \tag{4}
 \end{split}
\]
uniformly over the contact class.  If \(P\in\mathcal P^{\mathrm{int}}(\delta_0)\), the interior part of the same modulus instead gives
\[
 E_Pd_{\to}(C_n,\Theta_I(P))
 \leq \frac{C}{\sqrt{\delta_0}}E_PR_{n,P}+O(n^{-2})
 =O(n^{-1/2}). \tag{5}
\]
Equations (2), (4), and (5) use the identical \(C_n\) and multiplier critical value; no regime selector or additional tuning parameter has entered.  This proves the claimed adaptation.

theorem.statement:
The answer is affirmative.  Define
\[
 T_{n,P}=\sup_{\theta\in K}
 \frac{\sqrt n\{\widehat q_n(\theta)-q_P(\theta)\}}
      {\widehat\sigma_n(\theta)\vee n^{-1}},
 \qquad
 T_n^{\xi}=\sup_{\theta\in K}
 \frac{\widehat{\mathbb G}_n^{\xi}(\theta)}
      {\widehat\sigma_n(\theta)\vee n^{-1}}.
\]
Uniformly over \(P\in\mathcal P_W\), the bounded-Lipschitz distance between \(\mathcal L_P(T_{n,P})\) and the conditional law \(\mathcal L_\xi(T_n^{\xi}\mid W_1,\ldots,W_n)\) converges to zero in \(P\)-probability, and the conditional quantile is uniformly valid:
\[
 \liminf_{n\to\infty}\inf_{P\in\mathcal P_W}
 P\!\left\{
 \sup_{\theta\in\Theta_I(P)}
 \frac{\sqrt n\{\widehat q_n(\theta)-q_P(\theta)\}}
      {\widehat\sigma_n(\theta)\vee n^{-1}}
 \leq\widehat c_{1-\eta}\right\}\geq1-\eta. \tag{A}
\]
There are events \(\mathcal E_{n,P}\) and nonnegative random radii \(R_{n,P}\) such that
\[
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal P_W}P(\mathcal E_{n,P}^c)=O(n^{-2}),
 \qquad
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal P_W}E_P[R_{n,P}]=O(n^{-1/2}), \tag{B}
\]
the family \(\{\sqrt nR_{n,P}:n\geq1,P\in\mathcal P_W\}\) is uniformly integrable, and, on \(\mathcal E_{n,P}\),
\[
 C_n\subseteq\{\theta\in K:q_P(\theta)\leq R_{n,P}\},
 \qquad R_{n,P}\geq n^{-1/2}. \tag{C}
\]
Thus the same observable multiplier rule supplies simultaneous whole-identified-set calibration and an integrable root-\(n\) score-band radius on both \(\mathcal P_n^{\mathrm{ct}}(C_\delta)\) and \(\mathcal P^{\mathrm{int}}(\delta_0)\).
